SUN-EARTH SYSTEM IN THE GALAXY
First-order quasiperiodic formulation

TYPES AND FUNDAMENTALS
- Positions: vectors r(t) in a Galactic inertial frame
- Frequencies: angular frequencies (rad/time)
- Periods: T = 2*pi / omega
- Rationally independent frequencies => quasiperiodic motion
  (n1*omega1 + n2*omega2 + ... + nk*omegak = 0 has only the trivial integer solution)
- Scale separation: planetary motion is ~10^8 times faster than Galactic motion

1. SUN'S GALACTIC ORBITAL MOTION (planar)
T_gal = 2*pi * R_0 / v_odot

Variables:
  R_0     ~ 8 kpc                  (Sun's Galactocentric radius)
  v_odot  ~ 230 km/s               (circular speed at the Sun)
  T_gal   ~ 225-250 Myr            (galactic year; commonly ~230 Myr)

Number of orbits completed by the Sun:
  N ~ t_odot / T_gal ~ 4.6 Gyr / 230 Myr ~ 20

2. VERTICAL OSCILLATION OF THE SUN
Equation of motion (harmonic near mid-plane):
  d^2 z / dt^2 + omega_z^2 * z = 0

Solution:
  z(t) = A * sin(omega_z * t + phi)

Velocity and acceleration:
  v_z(t) = A * omega_z * cos(omega_z * t + phi)
  a_z(t) = - omega_z^2 * z(t)

Period and plane-crossing interval:
  T_z = 2*pi / omega_z ~ 60-70 Myr   (often ~64 Myr)
  T_cross = T_z / 2 ~ 30-35 Myr

Local density relation:
  omega_z ~ sqrt(4*pi*G*rho)
  T_z = sqrt(pi / (G*rho))

Vertical oscillations per galactic year: ~ 3-4

3. COMPOSITE SUN TRAJECTORY
r_odot(t) = r_planar(t) + z(t) * k_hat

Optional richer model with radial epicycles:
  R(t) = R_0 + B * cos(kappa * t + phi_R)
  r_odot(t) = R(t) * [cos(Omega_gal*t)*x_hat + sin(Omega_gal*t)*y_hat] + z(t)*z_hat

  kappa = epicyclic frequency
  (adds a fourth frequency)

4. PLANETARY ORBIT ABOUT THE SUN
In heliocentric frame: nearly Keplerian ellipse
For Earth:
  T_p = 1 yr
  v_orb ~ 29.8 km/s

Inclination of ecliptic to Galactic plane:
  i ~ 60.2 deg

5. LOCAL HELICAL APPROXIMATION
(valid only when carrier motion is treated as straight and uniform)

Axial advance per planetary orbit:
  L = v_odot * T_p
  (Earth: L ~ 48.5 AU)

Pitch ratio (local scale-separation parameter):
  P = v_odot / v_orb

Geometric meaning:
  P = L / (2*pi*a)
  (carrier distance traveled per orbit divided by one planetary circumference)

Examples:
  Mercury:  P ~ 4.85
  Earth:    P ~ 7.7
  Neptune:  P ~ 42.6

Helix angle:
  tan(alpha) = P
  (Earth: alpha ~ 82.6 deg)

Interpretation of P:
  P < 1     tight spiral
  P = 1     45 deg helix
  P > 1     stretched helix
  P >> 1    long corkscrew

6. FULL QUASIPERIODIC TRAJECTORY (Galactic frame)
Cleaner relative-coordinate decomposition:
  r_E(t) = r_odot(t) + r_E/odot(t)

or expanded:
  r_E(t) = r_G(t) + r_odot/G(t) + r_E/odot(t)

with
  r_odot(t) = r_planar(t) + A*sin(omega_z*t + phi)*z_hat
  r_E/odot(t) = R_tilt( r_Kepler(t) )

Three characteristic frequencies:
  Omega_gal = 2*pi / T_gal   ~ 2*pi / 230 Myr
  omega_z   = 2*pi / T_z     ~ 2*pi / 64 Myr
  omega_p   = 2*pi / T_p     ~ 2*pi / 1 yr   (Earth)

Scale separation:
  omega_p / Omega_gal ~ 2.3e8   (Earth)

The frequencies are rationally independent, so the trajectory is quasiperiodic.
It densely winds a torus in phase space.
Its projection into physical space appears as a non-closing tube-like path.

7. PHASE-SPACE DESCRIPTION
Motion is a linear flow on a 3-torus (or 4-torus with epicycles):
  theta_i(t) = omega_i * t + theta_i0
  T^3 = S^1 x S^1 x S^1

8. TIME-SCALE HIERARCHY
Planetary orbit      ~ 1 yr
Solar cycle          ~ 11 yr
Galactic crossing    ~ 32 Myr
Galactic orbit       ~ 230 Myr
Solar age            ~ 4.6 Gyr

The huge spacing between these scales supports hierarchical and perturbative 
descriptions.

