NOTES
- Pure helix requires idealized straight-line carrier motion.
- Real trajectory is a helix-on-a-curved-and-oscillating path.
- Frame dependence: closed ellipses (heliocentric) vs open corkscrews (Galactic).
- Neither frame is privileged; both describe the same motion.

DISTINCTIONS
Helix                      = local geometric approximation
Quasiperiodic trajectory  = global physical trajectory
Torus winding             = phase-space description
Reference frame           = coordinate choice

CONCEPTUAL SUMMARY
Planetary motion is locally well approximated by a helix,
globally described by a quasiperiodic trajectory,
and most naturally represented as a torus winding in phase space.

Hierarchy:
  Ellipse -> Helix -> Quasiperiodic Tube -> Torus Flow

